<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Melid</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 BCE – 712 BCE (approx.) · capital Melid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Melid, a Neo-Hittite kingdom at Malatya on the upper Euphrates, ruled by heirs of the Hittite royal house until Assyria annexed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Melid, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arslantepe">Arslantepe</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a>, had been an important town since the 4th millennium BCE. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> fell c. 1180 BCE, a dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzi-teshub">Kuzi-Teshub</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-carchemish">Kingdom of Carchemish</a> ruled the region, also called Kammanu, as an independent kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings wrote Luwian hieroglyphic inscriptions and adorned the gate of their citadel with reliefs of lions and of the king pouring offerings to the storm god, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. Melid lived from the farmland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> plain and the river crossing, paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarhunazi">Tarhunazi</a> in 712 BCE, made Melid an Assyrian province and gave part of it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kummuh">Kummuh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-hittite-states">Neo-Hittite</a> citadel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arslantepe">Arslantepe</a> was later abandoned.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/melid.jpg" alt="Melid" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Zeynel Cebeci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hittite_lions,_Arslantepe_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 BCE – 712 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Melid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sulumal, Gunzinanu, Tarhunazi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kammanu, Malatya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-946&amp;focus=melid" aria-label="Melid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5634 799 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Melid lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5954 1055L5954 1106M5954 1055L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5954 1106h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5954 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5954 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-946&amp;focus=melid">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
